--TEST--
Cancelling changes a job's state rather than removing its row, so the receipt still names something `status` can answer about — `rule:concurrency/queue-four-members` and `rule:concurrency/attempts-are-finite-and-a-dead-letter-is-kept`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A `delete` would have been the shorter statement and the worse member: a
// program that cancels a job and then asks what became of it would get a
// refusal, and "there is no such job" is exactly what a receipt this
// deployment issued is not allowed to mean. So `cancel` writes a state, and
// `Cancelled` is that state.
var $receipt = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs");

bool $won = Core\Queue::cancel($receipt);
Core\Queue\State $after = Core\Queue::status($receipt);
$after = Core\Queue\State::Cancelled;

// And it is a state like the other four, not a second kind of answer sitting
// beside them — which is what an `?int` or a separate `wasCancelled` would
// have made it.
bool $wrong = $after;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bool`, found `Core\Queue\State`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
